WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.866 --> 00:00:01.934
- Hey, gang.

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:03.503
Dave Baker here,
and these are some

00:00:03.569 --> 00:00:04.904
of my favorite short swords
featured on "Forged in Fire."

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:08.108
[music playing]

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:15.081
In close quarters,
in tight spaces,

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:18.351
often a long weapon is more
of a liability than a help.

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.486
That's why throughout
history, different cultures

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:23.690
have developed short swords to
fight in those tight spaces.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:33.433
This oddly bent
weapon is the sica.

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:39.706
Now, the sica was
originally a Thracian weapon

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:42.642
which was based on the
agricultural tool the sickle.

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:46.045
The Roman gladiatorial version
put the steep bend in it

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.181
so it could be used to
dig in and grab a shield

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:51.217
and allow that shield to
be pushed out of the way

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:52.719
before the attack is made.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:59.125
This double edged, heavy blade
was a devastating weapon.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:14.941
Now, this beautiful, slightly
curved weapon is the yatagan.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.743
The yatagan was
the primary weapon

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.511
of the Turkish Janissaries.

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.014
These weapons were in
use from the 14th century

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:23.382
to the 19th century.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.317
The slight curve
towards the front

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:28.454
makes it a fantastic
shopping weapon,

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:32.725
yet the point is developed
enough for the thrust.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:37.430
Many of these weapons had ivory
handles inlay mother of pearl.

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:40.333
Some of the most decorated
weapons from the period.

00:01:44.269 --> 00:01:47.573
It's a light, fast,
extremely mobile weapon.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:50.777
In the hands of a Janissary,
it would be terrifying.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:05.958
Now, this deadly blade is the
Roman gladius, one of the most

00:02:06.024 --> 00:02:08.094
iconic short swords in history.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.095
It's the sword of the
Roman legions, the sword

00:02:10.162 --> 00:02:11.798
that lasted 600 years.

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:19.839
Until recently, we haven't
had many actual pieces

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:20.973
that survived.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.375
What we've been
relying on of course

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:25.811
are either sculptures
or pottery depicting

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:27.212
soldiers using this weapon.

00:02:27.312 --> 00:02:29.082
Now, they're usually
depicted with the weapon

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:31.216
in the right hand
and a large shield

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:32.685
or scutum in the left hand.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.921
And that follows Roman
military doctrine,

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.256
which usually said
that you had to thrust

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:38.691
with the blade horizontal.

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:42.028
Now, that allowed it to go
up under or through armor

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:43.296
or between the ribs.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.034
The proof of this
weapon's effectiveness

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:51.571
is shown in the many years it
was used by the Roman legions.

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:08.320
This is the naval
cutlass designed

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.123
specifically for naval warfare.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.691
Back in the days
of the tall ship,

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:16.162
the ships were tight with
ropes everywhere, cannons.

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.197
There was very little
room to swing a weapon.

00:03:18.263 --> 00:03:20.399
A long blade would
be almost useless,

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:23.836
so a weapon was designed
specifically for that fight,

00:03:23.902 --> 00:03:25.471
and that weapon was the cutlass.

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:30.242
It had a lot of hand protection.

00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:32.311
You could deliver
blows with the guard.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:33.813
The blade was
shortened down so it

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:38.016
could be swung in close
quarters either with a hack

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.419
or used to stab.

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:44.256
This cutlass is based on
the design which was adapted

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:47.025
by the United States
military and still issued

00:03:47.092 --> 00:03:48.561
all the way to World War II.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:51.930
Close quarters, small
weapon, but a big punch

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:53.366
and one of my favorites.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:10.683
This is the xiphos, the
Greek warrior's sword.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:13.585
It had this leaf blade
with a wasp waist

00:04:13.685 --> 00:04:16.155
on it, which allowed
the weight to be forward

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.391
for serious chopping action.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.029
It had an acute
tip for thrusting.

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:25.098
[swords clanging]

00:04:25.198 --> 00:04:28.133
It had a guard that was
usually just big enough

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.369
to protect the hand
and a counterbalance

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:33.739
to balance all that weight out.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:36.375
These weapons spanned
hundreds of years of time

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:39.011
on the battlefield and
probably influenced a lot

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:42.414
of the gladius' development.

00:04:42.481 --> 00:04:44.750
Usually, the shield was
held in the left hand,

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.085
the xiphos in the right.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:47.619
And often, the
shield had a cutout.

00:04:47.686 --> 00:04:49.388
That allowed that
deadly, piercing point

00:04:49.488 --> 00:04:51.991
to be used to its best
effect, a weapon that

00:04:52.057 --> 00:04:53.960
truly passed the test of time.

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:00.666
So there you have
it, folks, some

00:05:00.732 --> 00:05:03.102
of my favorite short swords
featured on "Forged in Fire."

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:05.805
If you have any questions, put
them in the comments below,

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.905
and I'll try to get to
them in a future video.

